%[text] # Part 4 - Custom Board and Reference Design for the Digilent Arty A7-35T
%[text] **Goal:** teach HDL Coder about a board it does not know well, so that any Simulink DUT can be dropped into a ready-made Vivado design, built into a bitstream and talked to from MATLAB - all from the HDL Workflow Advisor GUI.
%[text] **Why a custom one?** HDL Coder ships a plugin called *Xilinx Artix-7 35T Arty development board*, but it targets the original Arty, uses `LVCMOS15` on 3\.3 V banks, labels the slide switches as push buttons, and its only reference design has no AXI bus (so MATLAB cannot read or write DUT registers). We build our own:
%[text] - correct Arty A7-35T part `xc7a35ticsg324-1L` and `LVCMOS33` pins for LEDs, slide switches, push buttons and RGB LED
%[text] - a reference design with the **HDL Verifier JTAG AXI Manager**: MATLAB becomes the AXI master over the same USB cable used for programming - no MicroBlaze, no software, no extra hardware \
%[text] Tested with: MATLAB R2026a, HDL Coder + HDL Verifier support packages for AMD FPGA and SoC devices, Vivado 2024\.1 (the version the R2026a tools recommend).
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Contents"}
%%
%[text] ## 0. What we are building
%[text] Board clock (100 MHz, pin E3) -> **Clocking Wizard** (50 MHz) -> **Processor System Reset** -> **JTAG AXI Manager** -> **AXI Interconnect** -> *your DUT IP core (inserted by HDL Coder)*. DUT ports that are not on AXI go straight to board pins (LEDs, switches).
%[text] Files (all already in this folder):
%[text:table]
%[text] | File | What it is | How it is made |
%[text] | --- | --- | --- |
%[text] | `arty_a7_35t/hdlcoder_board_customization.m` | Registers the board with HDL Coder | 3 lines |
%[text] | `arty_a7_35t/+ArtyA735T/plugin_board.m` | Board: FPGA part, JTAG position, pins | Fill-in-the-blanks template |
%[text] | `arty_a7_35t/+ArtyA735T/hdlcoder_ref_design_customization.m` | Lists reference designs for the board | 3 lines |
%[text] | `arty_a7_35t/+ArtyA735T/+jtag_axi_base/plugin_rd.m` | Reference design: where clock, reset and AXI connect | Fill-in-the-blanks template |
%[text] | `arty_a7_35t/+ArtyA735T/+jtag_axi_base/system_top.tcl` | The Vivado block design | **Exported from the Vivado GUI** (Step 1-2) |
%[text] | `arty_a7_35t/+ArtyA735T/+jtag_axi_base/arty_a7_35t_top.xdc` | Clock and reset pins | Copied from Digilent's master XDC |
%[text:table]
%[text] The `+` folders are MATLAB packages: `ArtyA735T.plugin_board` is how HDL Coder finds the board file.
%%
%[text] ## Where do the templates come from?
%[text] Nobody writes these files from a blank page. There is no "new board" wizard for IP Core Generation, but MathWorks ships complete, working board and reference-design folders that you copy and edit. The `arty_a7_35t` folder was put together from the sources below.
%[text:table]
%[text] | Source | What you get | How to get it |
%[text] | --- | --- | --- |
%[text] | Doc example [Define Custom Board and Reference Design for AMD Workflow](https://www.mathworks.com/help/hdlcoder/ug/define-and-register-custom-board-and-reference-design-for-zynq-workflow.html) | The official template: Digilent Zybo boards, `hdlcoder_board_customization.m`, board package, reference-design packages, exported `design_led.tcl`, step-by-step text | `openExample('hdlcoder/DefineCustomBoardAndReferenceDesignForZynqWorkflowExample')`; files are in `fullfile(hdlcoder_amd_examples_root, "ZYBO")` |
%[text] | Boards that ship with HDL Coder | Real, maintained plugins for many boards - including `+XilinxArty` (FPGA only, no processor), the closest starting point for an Arty | `fullfile(matlabroot, "toolbox", "hdlcoder", "boards", "amd")` |
%[text] | Reference designs that use the JTAG AXI Manager | The `addAXI4SlaveInterface(... 'MasterAddressSpace', 'hdlverifier_axi_manager_0/axi4m')` pattern and `addIPRepository` for the HDL Verifier IP | e.g. `+ZynqZC706/+vivado_ddr_2018_2` in the same `boards/amd` folder |
%[text] | More doc examples | Kria KV260 / KR260, ZCU104, Intel and Microchip boards | [Deploy IP Core on Custom Hardware](https://www.mathworks.com/help/hdlcoder/deploy-ip-core-on-custom-hardware.html) |
%[text] | Board vendor | Pin numbers and I/O standards | Digilent `Arty-A7-35-Master.xdc` |
%[text:table]
%[text] The folder structure of the official ZYBO template, which our `arty_a7_35t` folder mirrors:
%[text] ```
%[text] ZYBO/
%[text]   hdlcoder_board_customization.m            <- lists the board plugin(s); on the MATLAB path
%[text]   +ZyboZ710Registration/                    <- board package
%[text]     plugin_board.m                          <- hdlcoder.Board: device, pins, interfaces
%[text]     hdlcoder_ref_design_customization.m     <- lists the reference designs for this board
%[text]     +Vivado2022_1/                          <- one package per reference design
%[text]       plugin_rd.m                           <- hdlcoder.ReferenceDesign: Tcl, clock, AXI
%[text]       design_led.tcl                        <- Vivado File > Export > Export Block Design
%[text] ```
%[text] Quick start for a new board: copy the closest shipped board folder, rename the packages, then edit the values (part number, pins, Tcl file name, instance names).
src = fullfile(matlabroot, "toolbox", "hdlcoder", "boards", "amd", "+XilinxArty");
dir(src)
%[text] Reference pages: [Register a Custom Board](https://www.mathworks.com/help/hdlcoder/ug/register-a-custom-board.html) and [Register a Custom Reference Design](https://www.mathworks.com/help/hdlcoder/ug/register-a-custom-reference-design.html) document every property of `hdlcoder.Board` and `hdlcoder.ReferenceDesign`; `validateBoard` / `validateReferenceDesign` check your files.
%%
%[text] ## 1. Draw the block design in Vivado
%[text] This is ordinary IP Integrator work - the only MathWorks piece is the AXI Manager IP.
%[text] 1. Vivado 2024\.1 > **Create Project** > RTL project, *Do not specify sources*, part `xc7a35ticsg324-1L` (or the Arty A7-35 board if Digilent board files are installed).
%[text] 2. **Settings** > **IP** > **Repository** > **+** and add the HDL Verifier IP folder (the MATLAB line below prints the path).
%[text] 3. **IP Integrator** > **Create Block Design**, name it `system_top`.
%[text] 4. Add the four IPs in the table below with the **+** button and configure them.
%[text] 5. Right-click empty canvas > **Create Port**: `clk` (input, clock, 100 MHz) and `reset` (input, reset, polarity ACTIVE\_LOW).
%[text] 6. Wire it as in the picture. Leave `M00_AXI` of the interconnect **unconnected** - HDL Coder connects your DUT there. Validation may warn about it; that is expected \
%[text:table]
%[text] | IP | Settings |
%[text] | --- | --- |
%[text] | Clocking Wizard | Input 100 MHz, `clk_out1` = 50 MHz, reset type **Active Low** |
%[text] | Processor System Reset | Defaults |
%[text] | AXI Manager (vendor MathWorks, from the HDL Verifier repository) | Defaults |
%[text] | AXI Interconnect | 1 slave interface, 1 master interface |
%[text:table]
%[text] Path of the HDL Verifier IP repository for step 2:
fullfile(matlabshared.supportpkg.getSupportPackageRoot, "toolbox", "hdlverifier", ...
    "supportpackages", "fpgadebug_xilinx", "+hdlverifier", "+fpga", "+vivado")
%[text] ![Block design in Vivado IP Integrator](text:image:28f501)
%%
%[text] ## 2. Export the block design as Tcl
%[text] 1. **File** > **Export** > **Export Block Design...**
%[text] 2. Save it as `system_top.tcl` in `arty_a7_35t/+ArtyA735T/+jtag_axi_base/` \
%[text] ![File > Export > Export Block Design](text:image:30df02)
%[text] ![Export Block Design dialog](text:image:de6e03)
%[text] HDL Coder will re-run this Tcl in every project it creates, then insert the DUT. Your Vivado work is done.
%%
%[text] ## 3. Describe the board - plugin\_board.m
%[text] This file is declarative - you only fill in values. Key lines of `arty_a7_35t/+ArtyA735T/plugin_board.m`:
%[text] ```matlab
%[text] hB = hdlcoder.Board;
%[text] hB.BoardName    = 'Digilent Arty A7-35T (custom)';
%[text] hB.FPGAVendor   = 'Xilinx';   hB.FPGAFamily  = 'Artix7';
%[text] hB.FPGADevice   = 'xc7a35ti'; hB.FPGAPackage = 'csg324';  hB.FPGASpeed = '-1L';
%[text] hB.SupportedTool = {'Xilinx Vivado'};
%[text] hB.JTAGChainPosition = 1;
%[text] hB.addExternalIOInterface('InterfaceID','LEDs General Purpose', 'InterfaceType','OUT', ...
%[text]     'PortName','GPLEDs', 'PortWidth',4, 'FPGAPin',{'H5','J5','T9','T10'}, ...
%[text]     'IOPadConstraint',{'IOSTANDARD = LVCMOS33'});
%[text] hB.addExternalIOInterface('InterfaceID','Slide switches', 'InterfaceType','IN', ...
%[text]     'PortName','GPSW', 'PortWidth',4, 'FPGAPin',{'A8','C11','C10','A10'}, ...
%[text]     'IOPadConstraint',{'IOSTANDARD = LVCMOS33'});
%[text] % ... push buttons (D9 C9 B9 B8) and RGB LED LD0 (G6 F6 E1) the same way
%[text] ```
%[text] Every `addExternalIOInterface` becomes a choice in the **Target Platform Interfaces** column of the Workflow Advisor (Step 6).
%[text] *GUI alternative:* **Launch Board Manager** in task 1\.1 of the Workflow Advisor has a wizard for new boards, but those boards are for the FPGA-in-the-Loop and FPGA Turnkey workflows. IP Core Generation with a reference design needs the plugin files shown here.
%%
%[text] ## 4. Describe the reference design - plugin\_rd.m
%[text] This tells HDL Coder which Tcl to run and **where** in the block design to connect the DUT. The instance/pin names come straight from your Vivado diagram.
%[text] ```matlab
%[text] hRD = hdlcoder.ReferenceDesign('SynthesisTool', 'Xilinx Vivado');
%[text] hRD.ReferenceDesignName  = 'JTAG AXI Manager system';
%[text] hRD.BoardName            = 'Digilent Arty A7-35T (custom)';
%[text] hRD.SupportedToolVersion = {'2024.1'};
%[text] hRD.addCustomVivadoDesign('CustomBlockDesignTcl', 'system_top.tcl');
%[text] hRD.CustomConstraints = {'arty_a7_35t_top.xdc'};
%[text] hRD.addIPRepository('IPListFunction', 'hdlverifier.fpga.vivado.iplist');   % AXI Manager IP
%[text] hRD.addClockInterface( ...
%[text]     'ClockConnection',     'clk_wiz_0/clk_out1', ...
%[text]     'ResetConnection',     'proc_sys_reset_0/peripheral_aresetn', ...
%[text]     'DefaultFrequencyMHz', 50, 'MinFrequencyMHz', 10, 'MaxFrequencyMHz', 150, ...
%[text]     'ClockModuleInstance', 'clk_wiz_0', 'ClockNumber', 1);
%[text] hRD.addAXI4SlaveInterface( ...
%[text]     'InterfaceConnection', 'axi_interconnect_0/M00_AXI', ...
%[text]     'BaseAddress',         '0x40000000', ...
%[text]     'MasterAddressSpace',  'hdlverifier_axi_manager_0/axi4m', ...
%[text]     'InterfaceType',       'AXI4-Lite');
%[text] hRD.HasProcessingSystem         = false;   % no Arm / MicroBlaze
%[text] hRD.SupportedProgrammingMethods = hdlcoder.ProgrammingMethod.JTAG;
%[text] ```
%[text] Plus `arty_a7_35t_top.xdc` (pins `E3` clock, `C2` reset) and the two short registration files.
%%
%[text] ## 5. Register the board with MATLAB
%[text] HDL Coder looks for `hdlcoder_board_customization.m` on the MATLAB path. Add the folder (put it in `startup.m` to make it permanent) and, optionally, let HDL Coder check the files:
demoRoot = fileparts(matlab.desktop.editor.getActiveFilename);
addpath(fullfile(demoRoot, "arty_a7_35t"), fullfile(demoRoot, "models"));
validateBoard(ArtyA735T.plugin_board)
validateReferenceDesign(ArtyA735T.jtag_axi_base.plugin_rd)
%[text] Also tell HDL Coder where Vivado is (once per MATLAB session, or in `startup.m`):
hdlsetuptoolpath("ToolName", "Xilinx Vivado", "ToolPath", "C:\Xilinx\Vivado\2024.1\bin\vivado.bat");
%%
%[text] ## 6. Target the board from the HDL Workflow Advisor
%[text] The DUT in `models/fir_arty_ip.slx` reuses the Part 1 filter on AXI4-Lite registers and adds a small LED blinker: a 26-bit free-running counter, whose top 4 bits are XOR-ed with the slide switches.
open_system("fir_arty_ip")
%[text] ![Arty DUT test bench](text:image:f11f04)
%[text] ![Inside ArtyDUT](text:image:f7ea05)
%[text] Right-click `ArtyDUT` > **HDL Code** > **HDL Workflow Advisor**. Then go through the tasks top to bottom (right-click a task > **Run to Selected Task** runs everything before it).
%[text] **1\.1 Set Target Device and Synthesis Tool** - Target workflow `IP Core Generation`, Target platform `Digilent Arty A7-35T (custom)` (your board!), Synthesis tool `Xilinx Vivado`. Family, device, package and speed are filled from `plugin_board.m`.
%[text] ![1.1 Set Target Device](text:image:fcbf06)
%[text] **1\.2 Set Target Reference Design** - `JTAG AXI Manager system` from `plugin_rd.m`. The note says the AXI Manager is already in the design, so HDL Coder will not insert another one. *FPGA Data Capture* (also over JTAG) is offered for free as an on-chip logic analyzer.
%[text] ![1.2 Reference design](text:image:7b1507)
%[text] **1\.3 Set Target Interface** - map each DUT port: `x_in` and `y_out` to `AXI4-Lite` (register offsets `0x100`, `0x104`), `sw` to `Slide switches [0:3]`, `led` to `LEDs General Purpose [0:3]`.
%[text] ![1.3 Target interface](text:image:f8fb08)
%[text] **1\.4 Set Target Frequency** - 50 MHz (the Clocking Wizard is reprogrammed automatically if you change it).
%[text] **2\.1 Check Model Settings** - click **Modify All** to accept the HDL-friendly model settings.
%[text] ![2.1 Check Model Settings](text:image:ab1009)
%[text] **3\.2 Generate RTL Code and IP Core** - HDL Coder generates the Verilog, the AXI4-Lite register bank and address decoder, and packages everything as a Vivado IP with an HTML report of the register map.
%[text] ![3.2 Generate IP core](text:image:c03c10)
%[text] **4\.1 Create Project** - HDL Coder creates a Vivado project, runs your `system_top.tcl`, and connects the IP core to `axi_interconnect_0/M00_AXI` and to the board pins.
%[text] ![4.1 Create Project](text:image:340811)
%[text] **4\.2 Generate Software Interface** - with no processor, choose **Host target interface** = `JTAG AXI Manager (HDL Verifier)` and tick **Generate host interface script** (and/or model). HDL Coder writes the MATLAB code that talks to your registers for you.
%[text] ![4.2 Host interface](text:image:b93b12)
%[text] **4\.3 Build FPGA Bitstream** - synthesis, place & route, bitstream (around 5-10 minutes; **Run build process externally** keeps MATLAB free).
%[text] ![4.3 Build bitstream](text:image:a4c813)
%[text] Result of our build: timing met at 50 MHz (WNS +13\.4 ns), 630 LUTs, 903 flip-flops, 1 BRAM (FPGA Data Capture), **0 DSPs** - the CSD setting from Part 1 turned every coefficient multiply into shift-and-add.
%[text] **4\.4 Program Target Device** - connect the Arty's micro-USB cable and click **Run This Task** (programming method `JTAG`). The board shows up as `xc7a35t_0` on the Digilent cable.
%[text] ![4.4 Program](text:image:640914)
%[text] No board at hand? The bitstream from our build is in `arty_a7_35t/prebuilt/system_top_wrapper.bit`; program it from the Vivado Hardware Manager.
%%
%[text] ## 7. Talk to the DUT from MATLAB
%[text] Flip the slide switches - the LED blink pattern changes immediately (pure hardware). The filter registers are reached over JTAG through the AXI Manager. The essential calls are below (task 4\.2 can also generate a host interface script for you):
hFPGA = aximanager("AMD");                       % JTAG AXI Manager over the USB cable
x = fi(0.5, 1, 16, 15);                          % Q1.15
writememory(hFPGA, 0x40000100, int32(storedInteger(x)));    % x_in register (base 0x40000000 + 0x100)
pause(0.01)
yRaw = readmemory(hFPGA, 0x40000104, 1);         % y_out register, returned as uint32
y = double(typecast(uint32(yRaw), "int32")) / 2^18      % sign-extended Q4.18 -> real value
%[text] Note the `typecast`: `y_out` is a signed 19-bit value, sign-extended to 32 bits in the register, but `readmemory` returns it as `uint32`. Without the cast every negative value would read back as a huge positive number - the software equivalent of forgetting `$signed`.
%[text] A sweep of 40 values on our Arty A7-35T: every value read back from `y_out` equals the Q1\.15 value written to `x_in`. The filter's DC gain is 1, and it settles within 3 clocks (60 ns) of each register write, long before MATLAB reads the result.
vals = 0.9*sin(2*pi*(0:39)/40);
yRead = zeros(size(vals));
for k = 1:numel(vals)
    v = fi(vals(k), 1, 16, 15);
    writememory(hFPGA, 0x40000100, int32(storedInteger(v)));
    yRead(k) = double(typecast(uint32(readmemory(hFPGA, 0x40000104, 1)), "int32")) / 2^18;
end
release(hFPGA)
maxError = max(abs(yRead - double(fi(vals, 1, 16, 15))))
%[text] ![Values written over JTAG vs values read back from the FPGA](text:image:b1b515)
%[text] Because the AXI Manager is just an AXI master, the same MATLAB lines work for any register you map in task 1\.3 - coefficients, modes, status, capture buffers.
%%
%[text] ## Summary
%[text] - Vivado part: draw the block design once in the IP Integrator GUI and export it as Tcl.
%[text] - MATLAB part: two fill-in-the-blanks files (`plugin_board.m`, `plugin_rd.m`), two 3-line registration files, one XDC.
%[text] - From then on every Simulink DUT for this board is: Workflow Advisor > pick board > map ports > bitstream > program > access from MATLAB \
%%
%[text] ## How it all fits together
%[text:table]
%[text] | Part | What you did | Tool |
%[text] | --- | --- | --- |
%[text] | 1 | Equation -> Simulink model -> fixed point -> Verilog, plus an FSM in Stateflow | Simulink, Stateflow, Fixed-Point Designer, HDL Coder |
%[text] | 2 | Existing Verilog -> Simulink (import) or reused verbatim (BlackBox) | HDL Coder |
%[text] | 3 | Real RTL verified against the Simulink model, with Simulink or your own Verilog testbench driving it | HDL Verifier + Vivado Simulator |
%[text] | 4 | New board + reference design -> bitstream -> MATLAB access over JTAG on a real Arty A7-35T | HDL Coder, HDL Verifier, Vivado |
%[text:table]
%[text] The same FIR filter went through every step: designed in Simulink, compared bit for bit with hand-written Verilog, verified in cosimulation, and run on the FPGA, where the values read back over JTAG match exactly.

